Skia PDF: an OpenType/CFF face (PingFang SC on macOS/iOS, Noto Sans CJK on Linux, any .otf) is embedded as a CIDFontType0 / FontFile3 /CIDFontType0C subset, not as one Type3 font of per-glyph content streams. The Type3 fallback was forced because a CID-keyed CFF's charset (gid -> CID) is not the identity while the page content names glyphs by gid. SkPDFSubsetCFF (HarfBuzz subset, glyphs packed, no retained gids) rewrites the charset so packed glyph i maps to the original gid the content uses; a name-keyed CFF keeps its gids; a face it cannot handle (probed once per typeface, cached in the document) stays Type3. A 3-line zh/ja/ko page: 73 KB of Type3 -> 23 KB; pixels vs the Type3 render differ in 13 of 15554 ink pixels (MuPDF, 200 dpi); text, ToUnicode and widths unchanged; PDFKit renders it. TrueType / Latin-only PDFs are byte-identical.

